@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: how a run hands its terminal to the agent, and what it keeps alongside
' covers: internal/agentio/**
' covers: cmd/proveo/main.go
'
' Move 1 of _spec/_plans/main-decomposition.puml: lifted out of cmd/proveo/main.go
' with no behaviour change, proven by cmd/proveo/testdata/plan/*.golden. One thing
' the lift did NOT carry across unchanged: the move was cut before the interactive
' tail landed on main, so Stdio arrived here still returning nil on a terminal. It
' is restored below rather than back in main.go — the rule it belongs to is this
' package's.
'
' ── THE ONE RULE THAT SHAPES THIS PACKAGE ──────────────────────────────────
' os/exec grants a child the real terminal ONLY while the Stdout/Stderr field
' still holds that same *os.File. Any wrapper — an io.MultiWriter, a tee, a
' progress bar — silently degrades it into a pipe, and the agent then has no
' window size and draws its TUI one character per line. The rule binds the
' WRITERS, and only the writers: there is no "tee a little" option for them.
'
' ── THE RULE DOES NOT BIND THE TAIL ────────────────────────────────────────
' "Hands the terminal over unwrapped" and "takes no tail" were treated as ONE
' requirement, and conflating them is why a run that died at its prompt had no
' tail AND no transcript — "sandbox was stopped" was the entire report. They are
' not one requirement. Stdio therefore ALWAYS returns a Tail; on a terminal the
' writers stay untouched and the Tail is handed back empty, for internal/ptyproxy
' to fill from the master side via Proxy.OutTap. Those bytes already pass through
' proveo, so the copy costs the child nothing and `test -t 1` still succeeds
' inside. The run that forced this is written up in
' _spec/internal/runlog/run-transcript.puml.
'
' ── WHAT THE TAIL KEEPS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
' Tail retains the last 24 non-empty lines. It is a tee, not a filter: everything
' still reaches the destination untouched and only the retained copy is cleaned
' up for replay. The cleaning matters — a TUI's output is mostly escapes, and a
' tail full of cursor moves is harder to read than no tail at all:
'   * ANSI/OSC sequences stripped, remaining control bytes dropped (tab kept)
'   * consecutive identical lines collapse — a redrawn status line is ONE line
'   * an unterminated fragment is flushed on read, and capped at 8 KiB so a TUI
'     that never emits a newline cannot grow the buffer without bound
'
' ── THE MUTEX IS LOAD-BEARING ──────────────────────────────────────────────
' os/exec copies stdout and stderr on SEPARATE goroutines and both are teed into
' ONE Tail, so two Writes interleave on the same buffer: one goroutine computes an
' index, the other reslices under it, and the next reslice panics with bounds out
' of range. That is not a rare race — it took out the first real run this shipped
' in. TestTailIsConcurrencySafe reproduces it, and is worth running under -race.
'
' ── THE STDIN TRACE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
' PROVEO_TRACE_STDIN answers the question a transcript cannot: an agent that exits
' having "answered" prompts nobody typed is being driven by something on stdin — a
' multiplexer replying to a terminal query, a wrapper feeding a script, a stray
' paste. The transcript records what the agent RECEIVED; only a tap records what
' arrived, including what the filter DROPPED, which a child-side log can never show.
' Each read is one line: time, byte count, verdict, printable rendering, hex.
' Control bytes are the point — a terminal answering a device-attributes query
' looks like ordinary text in a transcript and like an escape sequence in the hex,
' and only the second tells the truth. Unset means byte-for-byte untraced, because
' the traced path swaps a plain pipe for a pty and that is not a change to make
' behind the operator's back.
title Agent terminal I/O — hand the terminal over AND keep the last words

participant "proveo run" as RUN <<app>>
participant "agentio.Stdio" as STDIO <<app>>
participant "agent process\n(docker run / sbx run)" as AGENT <<app>>
database "agentio.Tail\nlast 24 lines, de-escaped" as TAIL <<db>>
database "PROVEO_TRACE_STDIN file\nevery byte, sent or DROPPED" as TRACE <<db>>
participant "internal/ptyproxy\nterminal-report filter" as PROXY <<app>>

RUN ARROW_MAIN STDIO : (stdout, stderr, IsWriterTTY(stdout))

alt a terminal is attached
  STDIO ARROW_MAIN AGENT : the same *os.File, unwrapped
  AGENT ARROW_MAIN PROXY : the child's own pty master
  PROXY ARROW_QUEUE TAIL : Proxy.OutTap — copied off the master, in passing
else redirected, piped, or under tmux capture
  STDIO ARROW_MAIN AGENT : io.MultiWriter(dest, tail)
  AGENT ARROW_QUEUE TAIL : every line, as it streams
end

TAIL ARROW_QUEUE RUN : replayed when the run fails — from EITHER branch

RUN ARROW_OPTIONAL PROXY : FilterEnabled() && ptyproxy.Usable
PROXY ARROW_OPTIONAL TRACE : tap(bytes, forwarded)

note over STDIO
  Binary by construction for the WRITERS: os/exec
  hands over the terminal only by identity, so there
  is no "tee a little" option. Wrapping is what costs
  the agent its window size.

  The Tail is NOT part of that trade. It is always
  returned; on a terminal it comes back empty and the
  pty proxy is what fills it.
end note

note over TAIL
  A full-screen TUI restores the display as it
  exits, so a failed run can leave nothing on
  screen while every line passed through here.
  This is the copy the failure message replays.

  An interactive run reaches it by the OutTap branch
  above — the one channel it had none of while
  "unwrapped writers" and "no tail" were one rule.
end note

note over TRACE
  A child-side log shows what the agent read. Only
  a tap at this hop shows what the filter refused,
  which is the half that explains a session
  answering questions nobody typed.
end note

note over RUN
  IsStdinTTY also gates every interactive prompt —
  scope picker, env wizard, provisioning consent.
  One package answers "is this a terminal?" so two
  callers cannot disagree about it.
end note
@enduml
